lessons.body:
Most pages call a server. Two techniques deal with that, and both can go wrong if used casually.

**Waiting for a response**

When a visible signal is missing, wait for the response. Create the promise **before** the action that triggers it:

```ts
const responsePromise = page.waitForResponse(
  r => r.url().endsWith('/docs/intro') && r.status() === 200
);
await page.goto('https://playwright.dev/docs/intro');
const response = await responsePromise;
expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();
```

I ran this and it passes. A trap I also hit: on playwright.dev, clicking the "Get started" link did not fire a matching network response (the site switches pages in the browser), so `waitForResponse` waited 30 seconds and timed out. Wait only for a request that really happens. If you can assert on something visible, do that instead.

**Mocking: replace the server's answer**

`page.route` intercepts matching requests; `route.fulfill` answers them yourself. Playwright's docs describe this as replacing the real API call:

```ts
await page.route('**/api/shifts', route =>
  route.fulfill({ json: [{ id: 1042, status: 'filled' }] })
);
await page.goto('./');
const data = await page.evaluate(() => fetch('/api/shifts').then(r => r.json()));
expect(data).toEqual([{ id: 1042, status: 'filled' }]);
```

Register the route before the page makes the call. I ran this against the demo, using `fetch` from the page to stand in for the app's own call. A failure is mocked the same way:

```ts
await page.route('**/api/shifts', route => route.fulfill({ status: 500, body: 'boom' }));
```

Use that to check that the app shows a clear error when the server fails, which is hard to cause for real. `route.abort()` simulates a dropped connection. A HAR file can record and replay a whole set of calls.

**When to mock**

| Mock | Do not mock |
|---|---|
| A failure you cannot trigger (500, timeout) | The main path you want to prove end to end |
| A slow or paid third party (SMS, email) | Anything where the server logic is the point |
| Stable data for a visual check | Your own API on a smoke test |

A mocked test proves the front end handles the response. It does not prove the real server sends that response.

**Polling a value**

`expect.poll` retries a function until the value matches, replacing a hand-written wait loop. I used it to poll an HTTP status.

Playwright can call an HTTP API without a browser. The `request` fixture is a client that applies your config's `baseURL` and headers. Playwright's docs list three uses: check endpoints directly, set up server state before a UI test, and confirm server results after browser actions.

I ran these against a public practice API (jsonplaceholder.typicode.com), which fakes writes and does not save them:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('GET returns 200 and expected fields', async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.get('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1');
  expect(res.ok()).toBeTruthy();
  expect(res.status()).toBe(200);
  const body = await res.json();
  expect(body).toMatchObject({ id: 1, userId: 1 });
  expect(typeof body.title).toBe('string');
});

test('POST creates a record', async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.post('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts', {
    data: { title: 'Shift 1042', body: 'filled', userId: 1 },
  });
  await expect(res).toBeOK();
  expect(res.status()).toBe(201);
  expect(await res.json()).toMatchObject({ title: 'Shift 1042' });
});

test('missing record returns 404', async ({ request }) => {
  const res = await request.get('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/99999');
  expect(res.status()).toBe(404);
});
```

All three passed. Notes:

- `res.ok()` is true for status 200 to 299. Assert the exact status when it matters (201 for create, 404 for missing).
- `await expect(res).toBeOK()` is the web-first form for a response.
- `toMatchObject` checks the fields you name and ignores extras, which keeps the test from breaking when a new field is added.
- `res.json()` is asynchronous; await it.

**For the scheduling product**

Two uses make sense for a QA analyst. First, **check an endpoint** after a release: `GET /api/shifts/1042` returns 200 and `status: 'filled'`. Second, **create data fast**: instead of clicking through ten screens to make an open shift, POST it, then run a short UI test on it, and delete it afterwards.

**What this is not**

An API check does not replace UI tests, and it needs the team's API details: authentication, schema, test environment. Ask for those; do not guess endpoints.

Authentication usually means a header or token in `extraHTTPHeaders` or a login call. The token comes from a secret, as with the UI login.

